    written: [
      { q: "Expand the headline: 'Fire destroys market'.", a: "A fire has destroyed the market. (The simple present of the headline stands for a past event.)", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What does 'curb' mean in the headline 'Curb on noise'?", a: "'Curb' means a restriction or control; the headline means a restriction has been placed on noise.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Explain the headline 'PM to visit flood-hit state' in a full sentence and say what 'to visit' shows.", a: "The Prime Minister will visit the flood-hit state.\nThe infinitive 'to visit' shows a future action; the article and auxiliary ('The ... will') are restored.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Write a headline for each: (i) The police have arrested two robbers. (ii) The government will raise taxes.", a: "(i) Two robbers held (or: Police nab two robbers).\n(ii) Govt to hike taxes.\nArticles and auxiliaries are dropped; 'held' is a passive participle and 'to hike' shows the future.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Expand into full sentences: (i) Two held for theft (ii) Row over water sharing (iii) Probe into bridge collapse", a: "(i) Two people have been arrested for theft.\n(ii) A dispute has arisen over the sharing of water.\n(iii) An investigation has been started into the collapse of the bridge.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain three features of headline language with an example of each.", a: "1. Articles and auxiliaries are dropped: 'Fire destroys market' = 'A fire has destroyed the market.'\n2. The simple present stands for past events and 'to + verb' for the future: 'PM visits area' (past), 'Govt to open schools' (future).\n3. Short words and abbreviations replace long ones: probe, hike, bid, quit, PM, CM.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Rewrite in headline style: (i) The chief minister will inaugurate a new hospital. (ii) The price of petrol has been increased. (iii) Three students have been rescued from the river.", a: "(i) CM to inaugurate new hospital.\n(ii) Petrol price hiked.\n(iii) Three students rescued from river.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Expand these headlines into full sentences with the correct tense: (i) Floods hit state (ii) Schools to reopen on Monday (iii) Minister quits over scam (iv) Fuel prices hiked (v) Bid to save lake", a: "(i) Floods have hit the state.\n(ii) Schools will reopen on Monday.\n(iii) The minister has resigned over a scam.\n(iv) Fuel prices have been increased.\n(v) An attempt is being made to save the lake.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Write five rules for reading and expanding newspaper headlines, with an example for each.", a: "1. Articles and auxiliaries are omitted; restore them: 'Fire destroys market' → 'A fire has destroyed the market.'\n2. Simple present = a past event: 'PM visits flood area.'\n3. 'To + verb' = future: 'Govt to open schools' → will open.\n4. Past participle = passive: 'Two held' → two people have been arrested.\n5. Short words and abbreviations are replaced by ordinary ones: probe = investigation, hike = increase, CM = Chief Minister.", marks: 5 },
      {
        q: "Case study: A newspaper carries these headlines: (a) Cabinet to approve new highway (b) Heavy rain: schools shut (c) Probe into factory fire (d) Fuel prices hiked.\n(i) Which headline refers to the future?\n(ii) Which headline has a passive sense through a past participle?\n(iii) Expand headline (b).",
        a: "(i) (a), because 'to approve' shows a future action.\n(ii) (d), 'hiked' means 'have been increased'.\n(iii) Schools have been closed because of heavy rain.",
        marks: 4, type: "case"
      }
    ]
